## Exact input and proposed budgets

The base remains the pinned 80B-token pretrained checkpoint backed up in S3.
Its saved inventory declares 19 checkpoint files totaling 48,839,117,190 bytes
plus three tokenizer files totaling 4,648,253 bytes. Current S3 availability
was not checked. Eight trainer files account for 32,019,583,243 checkpoint
bytes: the frozen two-audit contract includes hashing them as opaque bytes,
but never deserializing them. They cannot silently be omitted.

| Item | Proposal, not measured or authorized execution |
| --- | --- |
| Import worker memory cap | 64 GiB |
| Host planning target | 128 GiB RAM, with at least 80 GiB MemAvailable before import; actual cgroup/host readback still required |
| Available disk target | 128 GiB; disk-backed private staging/output, not RAM-backed full-checkpoint storage |
| Logical coordinator reads | 200,055,533,069 bytes |
| Cumulative private content-write cap | 8,415,205,181 bytes, unchanged frozen ceiling |
| Import deadline | 3,600 seconds |
| HF per-file / shard target | 2 GiB / 256 MiB |
| Retries after a started import | Zero |

The resource algebra requires 8,413,764,005 private-write bytes and
74,438,839,808 staged/output/reserve disk bytes. The larger disk/host proposals
provide planning headroom; they are not measurements or reservations.

The 64 GiB DCP payload allowance and one-hour deadline are unmeasured proposals,
not guarantees of full-size serializer I/O sufficiency or performance.
The logical read/write caps exclude dependency-module and physical-device I/O.
Acquisition, staging copies, software extraction, host setup and aggregate
retention require their own combined budgets before execution approval.

An initial in-memory draft confused the aggregate HF output cap with a per-file
cap. The unchanged validator rejected it. The proposal now uses its existing
2 GiB per-file maximum; no guard, kernel policy or remote attempt was changed.

